(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-anatomy-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Systemic anatomy organizes the body according to what?","choices":["Functional systems (integumentary, musculoskeletal, nervous, etc.)","Discrete body regions (head/neck, thorax, pelvis)","The embryological origin of the tissue exclusively, without regard to adult location","Clinical practice specialties alone, without regard to body structure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Systemic anatomy is organized according to functional systems: integumentary, musculoskeletal, nervous, circulatory, respiratory, digestive, urinary, reproductive, and endocrine. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Regional anatomy is concerned with what?","choices":["All systems found in a discrete part of the body","Only the skeletal system across the whole body","Only the processes of clinical disease","Only the nervous system, whole body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Regional anatomy is concerned with all systems found in a discrete part of the body (head and neck, back, thorax and abdomen, pelvis, upper and lower extremities), with emphasis on relationships of systemic structures within that region. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In the anatomical position, how are the upper limbs and palms oriented?","choices":["By the sides with palms facing anteriorly","Raised overhead with palms facing medially","By the sides with palms facing posteriorly","Crossed over the chest with palms facing posteriorly"],"answer":0,"exp":"In the anatomical position, the upper limbs are by the sides with the palms facing anteriorly. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why are all anatomical descriptions expressed relative to the anatomical position?","choices":["Because it is the most comfortable position to stand in for long periods","Because it is required for radiographic imaging positioning only","To ensure descriptions are not ambiguous, regardless of the person's actual position","Because it is a position used solely in the context of surgical procedures"],"answer":2,"exp":"All anatomical descriptions are expressed in relation to the anatomical position to ensure the descriptions are not ambiguous, regardless of the actual position a person may be in. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What does the median (mid) sagittal plane do?","choices":["Passes in a horizontal orientation through the body, separating it into superior and inferior portions","Divides the body into upper and lower limb sections only","Passes vertically through the center of the body, dividing it into right and left sections","Passes in a vertical orientation through the body, separating it into anterior and posterior portions"],"answer":2,"exp":"The median (mid) sagittal plane is a vertical plane through the center of the body that divides it into right and left sections. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the coronal (frontal) plane do, and how does it relate to the median plane?","choices":["Divides the body into anterior and posterior sections, at a right angle to the median plane","Separates the body into anterior and posterior portions, running parallel to the median plane","Separates the body into right and left portions, running parallel to the median plane","Separates the body into superior and inferior portions, at a right angle to the median plane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The coronal (frontal) plane is a vertical plane that divides the body into anterior and posterior sections, at a right angle to the median plane. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the transverse plane do?","choices":["A horizontal plane dividing the body into superior and inferior sections","A vertical plane dividing the body into right and left sections","A vertical plane dividing the body into anterior and posterior sections","A horizontal plane dividing the body into anterior and posterior sections"],"answer":0,"exp":"The transverse plane is a horizontal plane passing through the body, dividing it into superior and inferior sections. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What do the terms \"proximal\" and \"distal\" describe?","choices":["Proximal and distal both describe left and right laterality of a structure","Proximal describes what is closest to the outer body surface; distal describes what is farthest from that surface","Proximal means anterior on the body; distal means posterior on the body","Proximal is closest to a point of reference (the body's center, in general use); distal is farthest from it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proximal describes an area closest to a point of reference (generally, closest to the body's center), while distal describes an area farthest from that point of reference. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What do the terms \"superficial\" and \"deep\" describe?","choices":["Superficial is closest to the body surface; deep is farthest from the surface","Superficial is closest to the body's center; deep is farthest from the center","Superficial means anterior; deep means posterior","Superficial and deep both describe movement at a joint"],"answer":0,"exp":"Superficial describes a point closest to the surface of the body, while deep describes a point farthest from the surface. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the difference between \"internal\" and \"external\" as anatomical terms?","choices":["They describe a structure's vertical superior/inferior position relative to another","They describe closest to/farthest from the surface","They describe the distance of a structure from the center of an organ","They describe a structure's anterior/posterior position relative to another"],"answer":2,"exp":"Internal and external describe the distance of a structure from the center of an organ. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the four primary tissue types in the human body, and their primary function each?","choices":["Epithelial (movement), connective (control), nerve (support), muscle (protection)","Epithelial (protection), connective (support), nerve (control), muscle (movement)","Epithelial (support), connective (protection), nerve (movement), muscle (control)","Only three tissue types exist: epithelial, connective, and muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The four tissue types are epithelial (primarily protection), connective (primarily support), nerve (primarily control), and muscle (primarily movement). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a defining structural feature of epithelial tissue regarding its composition and vascularity?","choices":["Consists almost entirely of cells with little extracellular material, and is avascular","Composed of roughly equal proportions of cells and extracellular matrix, and lacks blood vessels","Composed mostly of extracellular matrix with relatively few cells, and is richly supplied with blood vessels","Composed almost entirely of cells, and is richly supplied with blood vessels throughout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epithelial tissue consists almost entirely of cells with little extracellular material, and epithelial cells are avascular. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the basement membrane, and what is it composed of?","choices":["A thin layer of muscle tissue positioned directly beneath the epithelium","A richly vascular structure that supplies nutrients directly to the overlying epithelial cells","A nonliving adhesive substance secreted by epithelial cells, composed of connective tissue (collagen and glycoproteins)","A living cellular layer secreted and maintained by connective tissue cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"The basement membrane is a nonliving adhesive substance secreted by epithelial cells, similar to adhesive tape, composed of connective tissue elements (collagen and glycoproteins). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of epithelial tissue?","choices":["Secretes serous fluids to lubricate structures","Transmits nerve impulses to targets","Stores calcium and phosphate ions","Contracts its fibers in order to generate body movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epithelial tissue secretes serous fluids to lubricate structures, in addition to providing protection, filtering, secreting, and reabsorbing materials. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How is epithelial tissue classified by cell shape?","choices":["Areolar, adipose, and reticular tissue types, which are subtypes of connective tissue","Simple, stratified, and transitional layering only","Squamous (flat), cuboidal (as tall as wide), and columnar (tall and column-shaped)","Hyaline, fibrocartilage, and elastic cartilage types"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epithelial tissue is classified by cell shape into squamous (flat, \"scale\"-shaped), cuboidal (as tall as they are wide), and columnar (tall and column-shaped) cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the difference between \"simple\" and \"stratified\" epithelium?","choices":["Both terms describe the identical number of layers","Simple and stratified describe cell shape, not layer count","Simple has a single layer of cells; stratified has multiple or stacked layers","Simple has multiple layers; stratified has one layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Simple epithelium has a single layer of cells, while stratified epithelium has multiple or stacked layers. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is simple squamous epithelium found, and what is its function?","choices":["Used for absorption — found lining the walls of the digestive tract","Permeable, used for filtration and exchange — found in capillaries, alveoli, and kidney glomeruli","Used for protection — found lining the skin surface and the inside of the mouth","Used for secretion of enzymes — found lining the ducts of the salivary glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Simple squamous epithelium is permeable and used for filtration and exchange, found in locations like capillaries, alveoli, and kidney glomeruli. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is stratified squamous epithelium found, and what is its function?","choices":["Used for filtration — found in capillaries and kidney glomeruli","Used for reabsorption — found in kidney tubules","Used for movement — found in skeletal muscle","Used for protection — found in the skin and inside of the mouth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stratified squamous epithelium is used for protection, found in locations like the skin (keratinized) and the inside of the mouth. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Simple cuboidal epithelium is described as roughly cube-shaped and one cell thick. Where is it found?","choices":["Forming the multiple layers of the epidermis of the skin","Lining the mouth, throat, and esophagus as a lining","Lining the walls of blood vessels only, as seen in capillaries","Lining ducts in kidneys, where reabsorption and secretory activities take place"],"answer":3,"exp":"Simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts in kidneys and other locations where reabsorption and secretory activities take place. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is pseudostratified columnar epithelium, and where is it found?","choices":["A true multi-layered stratified tissue found only within the epidermis of the skin","A squamous-type tissue found lining the thin walls of capillaries and pulmonary alveoli","A cuboidal-type tissue found lining the collecting tubules of the kidney nephron","Gives the appearance of more than one layer of columnar cells despite being simple; found lining the nasal cavity, sinuses, and trachea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium gives the appearance of more than one layer of columnar epithelial cells (despite being simple), and lines locations like the nasal cavity, sinuses, auditory tubes, trachea, and bronchi. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are the two structural divisions of the nervous system, and what makes up each?","choices":["CNS (brain + spinal cord) and PNS (everything else)","Somatic (voluntary) and autonomic (involuntary) only","Sympathetic and parasympathetic only","CNS (brain only) and PNS (spinal cord + nerves)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The two structural divisions of the nervous system are the central nervous system (CNS), consisting of the brain and spinal cord, and the peripheral nervous system (PNS), which is everything else. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the three basic parts of the human brain?","choices":["Cerebrum, medulla, and pons only","Cerebellum, diencephalon, and spinal cord","Cerebrum, spinal cord, and brainstem","Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem"],"answer":3,"exp":"The three basic parts of the brain are the cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What structures make up the cerebrum, per the overview list?","choices":["Only the cerebral cortex and its overlying gyri and sulci, with absolutely no deeper structures included at all in this particular description","Diencephalon, midbrain, pons, medulla, and cerebellum structures only, but explicitly no cerebral hemispheres or lobes at all","Left and right hemispheres, lobes (frontal, parietal, occipital, temporal), gyri/sulci, longitudinal fissure, corpus callosum, basal nuclei, and limbic system","Only the frontal and parietal lobes specifically, with no other lobes, gyri, fissures, or deeper structures included at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cerebrum consists of left and right hemispheres, lobes (frontal, parietal, occipital, temporal), gyri and sulci, the longitudinal fissure, the corpus callosum, basal nuclei, and the limbic system. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the functional specializations of the left versus right cerebral hemispheres, per the material?","choices":["Both hemispheres have identical specializations","Left hemisphere controls only motor function; right controls only sensory function","Left: speech, writing, language, mathematics; Right: analysis by touch, spatial visualization","Left: analysis by touch, spatial visualization; Right: speech, writing, language, mathematics"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left hemisphere is specialized for the speech center, writing, language, and mathematics, while the right hemisphere is specialized for analysis by touch and spatial visualization. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the primary function of the frontal lobe?","choices":["Conscious perception of touch, pressure, and pain","Perception of visual stimuli","Perception of auditory and olfactory stimuli","Conscious control of skeletal muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"The frontal lobe is responsible for conscious control of skeletal muscles. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the primary function of the parietal lobe?","choices":["Conscious perception of auditory and olfactory stimuli exclusively","Conscious voluntary control of skeletal muscle movement and posture","Conscious perception of visual stimuli exclusively, with nothing else","Conscious perception of touch, pressure, vibration, pain, temperature, and taste"],"answer":3,"exp":"The parietal lobe is responsible for conscious perception of touch, pressure, vibration, pain, temperature, and taste. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the primary function of the occipital lobe, and the temporal lobe?","choices":["Occipital: conscious muscle control and movement; Temporal: touch and pressure perception","Occipital: perception of visual stimuli; Temporal: perception of auditory and olfactory stimuli","Occipital: perception of auditory stimuli only; Temporal: perception of visual stimuli only","Both lobes process only conscious voluntary motor function, with no sensory role involved at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"The occipital lobe handles perception of visual stimuli, while the temporal lobe handles perception of auditory and olfactory stimuli. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the precentral gyrus (motor strip), and what does it do?","choices":["Located within the temporal lobe entirely, not the frontal lobe; processes auditory stimuli and higher-order language comprehension functions directly","Located posterior to the central sulcus, not anterior to it; receives somatic sensory information for touch, pressure, and vibration sense","Located within the occipital lobe entirely, not the frontal lobe; processes visual stimuli and spatial depth perception directly and exclusively","Located anterior to the central sulcus; neurons direct voluntary movements by controlling somatic motor neurons in the brain stem and spinal cord"],"answer":3,"exp":"The precentral gyrus (motor strip) is anterior to the central sulcus, and its neurons direct voluntary movements by controlling somatic motor neurons in the brain stem and spinal cord. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the postcentral gyrus (primary sensory cortex), and what does it do?","choices":["Located posterior to the central sulcus; receives somatic sensory information for touch, pressure, pain, taste, and temperature","Located within the occipital lobe entirely, not the parietal lobe at all; processes visual data and spatial depth cues","Located anterior to the central sulcus, not posterior to it; directs voluntary skeletal muscle movement and posture","Located within the cerebellum entirely, not the cerebrum at all; coordinates balance and fine motor movement precisely"],"answer":0,"exp":"The postcentral gyrus (primary sensory cortex) is posterior to the central sulcus, and its neurons receive somatic sensory information for touch, pressure, pain, taste, and temperature from the dorsal columns and spinothalamic tract. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is gray and white matter organized within the cerebrum?","choices":["Only white matter is present throughout the entire cerebrum; gray matter exists solely within the cerebellum and brain stem structures instead","Gray matter (cerebral cortex) covers the surface; white matter lies underneath; deep gray matter masses (basal ganglia, diencephalon) sit at the midline","Gray and white matter are evenly and randomly distributed throughout the entire cerebrum, with no distinct layering or midline masses present at all","White matter covers the cortical surface entirely; gray matter lies underneath it instead, closer to the ventricular walls throughout"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cerebrum is covered with a layer of gray matter (the cerebral cortex), with white matter underneath the cortex, and deep gray matter masses (basal ganglia and diencephalon) in the midline of each hemisphere. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are the two structural divisions of the nervous system, and what does the PNS include?","choices":["CNS (brain and spinal cord) and PNS (everything outside the CNS, including nerves and ganglia)","CNS made up of the brain only; PNS made up of the spinal cord only, entirely separately","Somatic and autonomic divisions only, described structurally rather than functionally","Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions only, structurally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The two structural divisions are the CNS (brain and spinal cord) and the PNS (everything outside the CNS), with the PNS including nerves (spinal/peripheral and cranial nerves) and ganglia (neuronal cell bodies). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the two cell types that make up nervous tissue?","choices":["Only neurons; there is no supporting cell type","Neurons and osteocytes","Neurons and neuroglia","Neuroglia and chondrocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nervous tissue consists of two cell types: neurons (responsible for transfer and processing of information) and neuroglia (supporting cells that protect the neuron). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the function of dendrites, per the material?","choices":["Affect another neuron or an effector organ directly","Stimulated by environmental changes or the activities of other cells/neurons","Conduct the nerve impulse toward the axon terminals","Contain the nucleus and mitochondria of the cell body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dendrites are stimulated by environmental changes or the activities of other cells/neurons. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the cell body of a neuron contain?","choices":["Only the axon hillock region of the neuron, a narrow transitional zone near the cell body","The nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes, and other organelles and inclusions","Only the axon terminals of the neuron, located far from the cell body","Only the myelin sheath covering the neuron's axon, formed by neighboring glial cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cell body contains the nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes, and other organelles and inclusions. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the axon do, and what do axon terminals do?","choices":["The axon is stimulated by environmental changes; axon terminals contain the neuron's nucleus","The axon terminals conduct the impulse; the axon affects the target organ instead","The axon conducts the nerve impulse toward axon terminals; axon terminals affect another neuron or effector organ (muscle or gland)","The axon and the axon terminals are said to perform an identical, fully interchangeable function"],"answer":2,"exp":"The axon conducts the nerve impulse (action potential) toward the axon terminals, which affect another neuron or an effector organ (muscle or gland). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are multipolar neurons, and where are they found?","choices":["Have multiple (2+) cellular processes joining the cell body; abundant in both the CNS and PNS","Possess two cellular processes each; account for a few of the special sensory pathways","Possess a single process that later bifurcates; commonly found in the peripheral nervous system","Have no distinguishable axon or dendrites present anywhere on the cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Multipolar neurons have multiple (2+) cellular processes joining the cell body and are abundant in both the central and peripheral parts of the nervous system. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are pseudounipolar neurons, and where are they common?","choices":["Possess two processes with the cell body positioned between them; found within the special senses","Common in the CNS; have multiple processes joining the cell body directly","Common in the PNS; have one cellular process connected to the cell body that subsequently bifurcates into two processes","Have no distinguishable axon at all; found only within the retina of the eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pseudounipolar neurons are common in the PNS and have one cellular process connected to the cell body that subsequently bifurcates into two processes. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are bipolar neurons, and where are they found?","choices":["Have one process that bifurcates; common in the PNS","Have multiple (2+) processes; abundant in the CNS and PNS generally","Have processes where axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites","Have two cellular processes; make up a few of the special senses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bipolar neurons have two cellular processes and make up a few of the special senses. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What distinguishes afferent neurons from efferent neurons?","choices":["Afferent and efferent describe neuron shape, not impulse direction","Afferent neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS; efferent neurons conduct impulses toward the CNS","Both conduct impulses in the same direction","Afferent neurons conduct impulses toward the CNS; efferent neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS"],"answer":3,"exp":"Afferent neurons conduct impulses toward the CNS, while efferent neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the difference between somatic and visceral neurons?","choices":["Somatic neurons convey information to and from somatic structures; visceral neurons innervate visceral structures","Somatic and visceral describe impulse direction, not target structure type","Both terms describe the exact same category of neuron entirely, with no functional distinction","Somatic neurons exclusively innervate the heart, while visceral neurons exclusively innervate skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Somatic neurons convey information to and from somatic structures, while visceral neurons innervate visceral structures. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How long is the spinal cord, and to what vertebral level does it extend?","choices":["20 cm; extends from the brain to approximately L4","100 cm; extends from the brain to the sacrum","45 cm; extends from the brain to approximately L2","45 cm; extends from the brain to approximately T12"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spinal cord is 45 cm in length and extends from the brain to approximately L2, passing through the foramen magnum. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What arteries supply the spinal cord, per the gross anatomy overview?","choices":["Only the vertebral arteries directly, with no dedicated spinal arteries","Two anterior spinal arteries and one posterior spinal artery","One anterior spinal artery and two posterior spinal arteries","Four spinal arteries, two anterior and two posterior"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spinal cord is fed by one anterior spinal artery and two posterior spinal arteries. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the conus medullaris, and where does it typically end?","choices":["A bundle of nerve roots extending below the spinal cord's true end","A ligament connecting the spinal cord to the coccyx","The beginning of the spinal cord at the foramen magnum","The true end of the spinal cord, typically ending at L1-L2"],"answer":3,"exp":"The conus medullaris is the true end of the spinal cord, typically ending at L1-L2. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the cauda equina, and what does it innervate?","choices":["The enlarged region of the spinal cord found in the cervical area, housing upper limb motor neurons","The true, tapered end of the spinal cord itself, located at approximately L1-L2","Nerve roots extending from the conus medullaris to innervate pelvic and lower extremity structures","A thin ligament-like extension that becomes part of the coccygeal ligament inferiorly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cauda equina consists of nerve roots that extend from the conus medullaris to innervate pelvic and lower extremity structures. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the filum terminale, and what does it become?","choices":["A structure that becomes a component of the coccygeal ligament","The true, tapered end of the spinal cord, called the conus medullaris","A bundle of nerve roots innervating the lower extremities","The widened region of the spinal cord in the lumbosacral area"],"answer":0,"exp":"The filum terminale becomes a component of the coccygeal ligament. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the difference between the dorsal root and ventral root of a spinal nerve?","choices":["Dorsal root is motor, controlling limb muscles directly; ventral root is sensory, relaying pain and temperature","Dorsal root carries only autonomic fibers to visceral organs; ventral root carries only somatic sensory fibers","Dorsal root is sensory, with axons extending from the soma within the dorsal root ganglion; ventral root is motor","Dorsal root is motor, with cell bodies in the anterior gray horn; ventral root is sensory"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dorsal root is sensory, with axons extending from the soma within the dorsal root ganglion, while the ventral root is motor; the two merge to form a spinal nerve. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does the gray matter of the spinal cord consist of, and where is it located relative to the central canal?","choices":["Only myelin, with no cell bodies","Axons organized into tracts, located outside the gray matter area","Somas (cell bodies/nuclei) surrounding the central canal","Only glial cells, with no neurons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gray matter consists of somas (cell bodies; nuclei) surrounding the central canal, which supplies CSF. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the white matter of the spinal cord consist of, and how are its nerves organized?","choices":["Consists of axons, organized into tracts or columns (generally termed fascicles), located outside the gray matter","Consists of cerebrospinal fluid only, circulating freely within the central canal","Consists of neuron cell bodies and their dendrites, arranged surrounding the central canal region","Consists only of dorsal root ganglion tissue, located just outside the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"White matter consists of axons, organized into tracts or columns (generally termed fascicles), located outside the gray matter area. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do the posterior gray horns, lateral gray horns, and anterior gray horns each contain?","choices":["Posterior: visceral motor nuclei only; Lateral: somatic motor nuclei only; Anterior: somatic sensory nuclei only","Posterior: somatic motor nuclei only; Lateral: visceral sensory nuclei only; Anterior: somatic sensory nuclei only","Posterior: somatic motor nuclei; Lateral: somatic sensory nuclei; Anterior: visceral motor nuclei only","Posterior: somatic sensory/visceral nuclei; Lateral: visceral motor nuclei; Anterior: somatic motor nuclei"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior gray horns contain somatic sensory and visceral nuclei, the lateral gray horns contain visceral motor nuclei (present T1-L2, controlling smooth muscle, heart, and glands), and the anterior gray horns contain somatic motor nuclei. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In the anterior gray horn, how are extensor and flexor motor neurons arranged?","choices":["Extensors are lateral; flexors are medial only","Both are located in identical positions","Extensors are more anterior/rostral; flexors are more posterior/caudal","Extensors are more posterior/caudal; flexors are more anterior/rostral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Within the anterior gray horn, extensor motor neurons are positioned more anterior/rostral, while flexor motor neurons are positioned more posterior/caudal. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What two branches do all spinal nerves form?","choices":["Superior ramus and inferior ramus, which divide at each intervertebral level","Only a single ventral ramus, with no corresponding dorsal branch","Dorsal (posterior) ramus and ventral (anterior) ramus","White ramus communicans and gray ramus communicans only"],"answer":2,"exp":"All spinal nerves form two branches: the dorsal (posterior) ramus and the ventral (anterior) ramus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which spinal nerves have two additional branches (white and gray rami), and what are these rami collectively called?","choices":["Spinal nerves T1 to L2; collectively called rami communicantes","Spinal nerves S1 to S5; collectively called the sacral plexus","All spinal nerves have these additional branches","Spinal nerves C1 to C5; collectively called the cervical plexus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Spinal nerves T1 to L2 have two additional branches — the white ramus and gray ramus — which are collectively called rami communicantes. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are white and gray rami associated with, in the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["No association with the ANS","The autonomic ganglion of the sympathetic division","The enteric nervous system exclusively","The autonomic ganglion of the parasympathetic division"],"answer":1,"exp":"White and gray rami are associated with the autonomic ganglion of the sympathetic division within the autonomic nervous system. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are dermatomes?","choices":["A classification scheme used to categorize different reflex arc types","A specific branch given off by each of the twelve cranial nerves","Regions of skeletal muscle supplied by a given spinal nerve, called a myotome","Specific regions of the skin supplied by a given spinal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Spinal nerves supply specific regions of the skin known as dermatomes. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In the distribution of motor fibers within a spinal nerve, what do the white ramus (preganglionic) and gray ramus (postganglionic) carry?","choices":["White ramus carries postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve; gray ramus carries preganglionic fibers to the sympathetic ganglion","White ramus carries somatic motor fibers to skeletal muscle; gray ramus carries somatic sensory fibers back to the spinal cord","White ramus carries parasympathetic fibers only; gray ramus carries no fibers at all, serving as a structural attachment","White ramus carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the sympathetic ganglion; gray ramus carries postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The white ramus carries preganglionic fibers to the sympathetic ganglion, while the gray ramus carries postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve for distribution to smooth muscles, glands, and visceral organs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the distribution of sensory fibers within a spinal nerve, what information does the dorsal ramus carry versus the ventral ramus?","choices":["Dorsal ramus carries sensory information from exteroceptors/proprioceptors/interoceptors of the back; ventral ramus carries the same from the body wall and limbs","Dorsal ramus carries only proprioceptive information from muscle spindles; ventral ramus carries only nociceptive information from visceral organs","Dorsal ramus carries motor information to the deep back muscles only; ventral ramus carries sensory information from the limbs only","Dorsal ramus carries only visceral sensory information from thoracic and abdominal organs; ventral ramus carries only somatic sensory information from the limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"The dorsal ramus carries sensory information from exteroceptors, proprioceptors, and interoceptors of the back, while the ventral ramus carries sensory information from exteroceptors, proprioceptors, and interoceptors of the body wall and limbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is a nerve plexus, and what are the four major nerve plexuses?","choices":["A single unbranched peripheral nerve; the radial, ulnar, median, and femoral nerves are examples","Only two plexuses exist in the body: the cervical plexus and the lumbar plexus","A type of autonomic ganglion found only among the twelve cranial nerves","An interwoven network of nerves (\"plexus\" means \"to braid\"); the cervical, brachial, lumbar, and sacral plexuses"],"answer":3,"exp":"A nerve plexus is an interwoven network of nerves (\"plexus\" meaning \"to braid\"); there are four nerve plexuses — cervical, brachial, lumbar, and sacral (the lumbar and sacral are sometimes combined into the lumbosacral plexus). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What spinal nerve levels form the cervical plexus, and what does it innervate?","choices":["C5-T1; innervates the muscles of the shoulder and upper limb","C1-C5; innervates the muscles of the neck and the diaphragm","T12-L4; innervates the pelvic girdle and anterior thigh","L4-S4; innervates the muscles of the lower limb and buttock"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cervical plexus (C1-C5) innervates the muscles of the neck and the diaphragm. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which nerve, arising from the cervical plexus, innervates the diaphragm?","choices":["Great auricular nerve, arising from the C2 and C3 spinal nerve levels","Ansa cervicalis, formed by the union of C1 through C3 spinal nerve fibers","Lesser occipital nerve, arising solely from the C2 spinal nerve level","Phrenic nerve (originating mainly from C4, with contributions from C3 and C5)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The phrenic nerve, which provides sensory information from and motor innervation to the diaphragm, originates from C4 with minor contributions from C3 and C5. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which nerve of the cervical plexus innervates five of the extrinsic laryngeal muscles, traveling with fibers from CN XII?","choices":["Phrenic nerve","Ansa cervicalis","Great auricular nerve","Transverse cervical nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ansa cervicalis originates from branches of C1-C3 (and sometimes C4) and travels inferiorly with fibers from cranial nerve XII, innervating five of the extrinsic laryngeal muscles. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
